@page "/page-recipes"

<CatHead Title="Page recipes" Tier="@CatHead.Tier2">
    Whole pages, composed from the classes on the other pages: an overview, a list and the record it
    leads to, the forms that create and change one, and the pages around them. Copy one whole and
    change its content.
</CatHead>

<div class="callout callout--info">
    <i class="ri-information-line"></i>
    <div class="callout-body">
        <span class="callout-title">The code is the page; the frame around it is Shell</span>
        Each recipe is drawn inside the app's frame &mdash; a sidebar, here folded to its icon rail,
        the topbar and <code>.page</code> &mdash; so it can be read as the page it is. The code under
        it is only what goes <em>inside</em> <code>.page</code>: take the frame once, from
        <a href="/frame">Shell</a>, and put a recipe in it. The sign-in page is the one exception,
        and its code is the content of <code>.auth-layout</code>.
    </div>
</div>

<p class="cat-lede">Choose by what the reader came to do.</p>
<div class="card">
    <table class="table">
        <thead><tr><th>Recipe</th><th>The reader came to</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><a href="#an-overview">Overview</a></td><td>See how things stand, and what needs them</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a href="#a-list-page">List page</a></td><td>Find one record among many</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a href="#a-list-page-while-it-loads">List while it loads</a></td><td>The same, before the rows have arrived</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a href="#a-list-with-nothing-in-it-yet">Empty list on first visit</a></td><td>Start, on their first visit</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a href="#search-results">Search results</a></td><td>Find something whose kind they do not know</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a href="#a-detail-page">Detail page</a></td><td>Read one record and act on it</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a href="#a-create-or-edit-page">Create or edit page</a></td><td>Make a record, or change one</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a href="#a-step-in-a-longer-task">Step in a longer task</a></td><td>Do something that takes several decisions</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a href="#a-list-beside-its-record">List beside its record</a></td><td>Work through many records one after another</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a href="#a-settings-page">Settings page</a></td><td>Change how the app behaves for them</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a href="#an-account-page">Account page</a></td><td>See and change what the app knows about them</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a href="#a-sign-in-page">Sign-in page</a></td><td>Get in</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<CatGroup Title="Overviews and lists">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.PageRecipes.Overview" Title="Overview" Shell="Overview">
        <p>
            The figures first, in one card so the row of <code>.stat</code> tiles reads as one object; then
            the two charts on a <a href="/grid"><code>.sedna-grid-24</code></a>, and under them the two
            lists that send the reader somewhere: what needs them, and what just happened. Every panel
            ends in a link to the page it summarises. The period control is in the page head because it
            applies to the whole page &mdash; a control inside one card would read as that card's alone.
        </p>
        <p>
            The charts are <a href="/chart"><code>[data-chart]</code></a> tables. On a narrow screen the
            grid stacks its panels in source order, so put them in the order a phone reader should meet them.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.PageRecipes.ListPage" Title="List page" Shell="Orders">
        <p>
            Page head, toolbar, the filters that are applied, the table in a card, the pager &mdash; each a
            child of <code>.page</code>, so the page's own rhythm spaces them and no margin is written by
            hand. The head's actions fold into a menu on a narrow screen and keep the primary one; the
            toolbar keeps the filters on the leading side and the count on the trailing one; the chips say
            what is applied and take it off in one press. On a phone the table stacks into one card per row.
        </p>
        <p>
            The count and the pager agree: the toolbar says how many rows match, the pager how many of
            those are on this page. When nothing matches, the table keeps its head and shows one
            <code>.tr-empty</code> row, as on <a href="/table">Tables</a> &mdash; a filter that found
            nothing is not an empty page.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.PageRecipes.LoadingPage" Title="List while it loads" Shell="Orders">
        <p>
            The page keeps everything it knows before the rows arrive: the head, the toolbar, the table's
            own columns. Only the rows are <a href="/skeleton"><code>.skeleton</code></a> lines, shaped like
            the values they stand for, and the count says it is loading. Swapping the whole page for a
            spinner moves every control the reader was reaching for when the data lands.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.PageRecipes.FirstRun" Title="Empty list on first visit" Shell="Customers">
        <p>
            The first visit, before there is anything to list. The page head stays &mdash; the reader
            still needs to know where they are &mdash; and the table gives way to an
            <a href="/empty-state"><code>.empty-state</code></a> that says what will be here and offers the
            ways to start, the likelier one primary. Two cards under it answer what the reader asks next.
            Use this only when nothing exists yet; a search or a filter that finds nothing keeps the table.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.PageRecipes.SearchResults" Title="Search results" Shell="Search">
        <p>
            The query stays in the field it was typed into, so it can be corrected rather than retyped.
            The tabs split the results by kind with a count on each, the toolbar narrows them further, and
            each row says what kind of thing it is and why it matched. The kind and the state sit on a
            line of their own above the title, where they wrap on a phone instead of squeezing it. The count is a live region: the
            reader hears how many results a change produced.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>

<CatGroup Title="Records">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.PageRecipes.DetailPage" Title="Detail page" Shell="Orders">
        <p>
            The trail back to the list, a head naming the record with its state beside the name, and an
            <a href="/alert"><code>.alert</code></a> for the one thing the reader has to know before acting.
            The <a href="/tabs">tab strip</a> is links, one address per part of the record. Under it
            <code>.sedna-split</code>: the record's fields and lines in the main column, the people and the
            history in the aside, which drops under the main column when the split runs out of room.
        </p>
        <p>
            Fields are <code>.kv</code> rows, <code>.kv--text</code> where the value is prose. A badge
            inside the <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> is read as part of the name, so keep it to the state.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.PageRecipes.FormPage" Title="Create or edit page" Shell="Orders" ShellHeight="680px">
        <p>
            <strong>Live &mdash; scroll the page.</strong> One form in one card, in
            <code>.form-section</code>s that each name a subject, with fields side by side only where they
            belong together. After a rejected submit the summary at the top names every field that needs
            attention and links to it, and each field says what is wrong in its own words. The actions
            stay in reach while the form is longer than the screen &mdash;
            <a href="/form-saving"><code>.form-actions--sticky</code></a> &mdash; with the draft on the far
            side from the commit button.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.PageRecipes.WizardStep" Title="Step in a longer task" Shell="Orders">
        <p>
            Use it when a later question depends on an earlier answer. The
            <a href="/collections"><code>.steps</code></a> row says where the reader is and how far is left;
            the card asks one question, and Continue is its only button. The
            <a href="/frame"><code>.back-link</code></a> above the head goes to the previous step, never to
            the browser's history. An option that cannot be
            chosen says why where it is shown, rather than disappearing. For a long form a reader fills in
            over several sittings, use a task list instead &mdash; see
            <a href="/form-flow">Multi-page forms</a>.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.PageRecipes.MasterDetail" Title="List beside its record" Shell="Messages">
        <p>
            For working through records one after another: the list holds its place while the record
            beside it changes, and the open one is marked with <code>aria-current</code>. The list is the
            split's aside, so it keeps its width and the record takes the rest; where the two no longer
            fit, the record drops under the list &mdash; an app that shows one at a time on a phone
            routes between them instead. Each half is a card with its own
            head, so the actions in the record's head plainly belong to that record.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>

<CatGroup Title="Account, settings and feedback">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.PageRecipes.SettingsPage" Title="Settings page" Shell="Settings" ShellHeight="640px">
        <p>
            <strong>Live &mdash; scroll the page, or follow a link in the contents.</strong> The
            <a href="/page-navigation"><code>.page-toc</code></a> stays in view beside the sections and marks
            the one being read. One boxed <code>.form-section</code> per subject, each with a line saying
            what it is for; what most readers never change folds away. A switch applies a choice; Save
            applies the fields &mdash; if a switch should wait for Save too, make it a checkbox.
        </p>
        <p>
            The save bar shows only while the form differs from what was saved: the app puts
            <code>data-dirty</code> on the form while it does, and takes it off once saved. It is in the
            markup here so the bar can be seen.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.PageRecipes.AccountPage" Title="Account page" Shell="Account">
        <p>
            What the app knows about the reader, as <code>.kv</code> rows with a change link each, and a
            row with nothing to change where the reader cannot change it. The sessions table names the
            device the reader is on, so they do not sign themselves out. Leaving is last and alone in its
            card, and says what happens to the reader's work before the button does it.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.PageRecipes.SignIn" Title="Sign-in page" AuthShell="true">
        <p>
            The content of <a href="/frame"><code>.auth-layout</code></a>: no navigation, because there is
            nothing to navigate to yet. A failed attempt says so above the fields without saying which one
            was wrong, and keeps the e-mail address. The <code>autocomplete</code> values are what let a
            password manager fill both fields.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.PageRecipes.PageFeedback" Title="Page feedback" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            Not a page but the end of one, after its content: one question and two answers.
            <strong>Yes</strong> records the answer and replaces the row with the thanks;
            <strong>No</strong> and &ldquo;Report a problem&rdquo; replace it with a short form of two open
            questions, and Send replaces the form with the thanks. All three are one region whose content
            the app swaps, so focus and the reader's place stay where they were; send the page's address
            with the answer. Ask for nothing personal, and say so above the fields.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>
